window.ME_DATA = window.ME_DATA || {};
window.ME_DATA["y11-me"] = [
 {
  id:"l1", lesson:1, title:"What Do \"Economy\" and \"Globalisation\" Mean?", unit:"Unit 1: Ethics and the Global Economy", art:"globe",
  dna:"Look at the labels on three things you have with you today (clothes, phone, stationery, food packaging). Write down where each was made or grown. What does this tell you about how connected the world's economies are?",
  dnaQs:["Where were three of the items you have with you today made or grown?","How many different countries were involved?","What does this tell you about how connected the world's economies are?"],
  dnaHint:"A good answer names real countries from the labels (for example, a phone assembled in one country using parts from several others), notices that very few everyday items are made entirely in one place, and links this to the idea that economies are interdependent: what happens in one country affects others.",
  intentions:["Understand what is meant by 'economy' and 'globalisation'.","Explore the main drivers of globalisation, such as trade, technology, transport and communication.","Analyse how the UAE's economy is connected to the global economy."],
  criteria:["I can define 'economy' and 'globalisation' accurately in my own words.","I can explain at least three drivers of globalisation with examples.","I can analyse how the UAE participates in, and depends on, the global economy."],
  concept:{key:"Global interactions", related:"Interdependence, Systems", soi:"Economic systems are increasingly interdependent, so the choices of individuals, businesses and nations have consequences far beyond their own borders.", context:"Globalisation and Sustainability — exploring how trade, technology and communication connect people and economies across the world.", uae:"The UAE's role as a global trade and logistics hub (Jebel Ali Port, Dubai and Abu Dhabi airports), its economic diversification beyond oil, and its Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreements (CEPAs); links to Individuals & Societies (Economics) and Geography."},
  words:[["Economy","The system by which a country or region produces, distributes and consumes goods and services."],["Globalisation","The process by which economies, cultures and people around the world become increasingly connected and interdependent."],["Interdependence","A situation in which countries or people rely on each other, so that changes in one affect the others."],["Trade","The buying and selling of goods and services, including between countries (imports and exports)."],["Supply chain","The sequence of steps and places involved in producing a product and delivering it to the consumer."],["Diversification","Expanding an economy into a wider range of sectors to reduce reliance on a single one, such as oil."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Choose ONE everyday product (e.g. a smartphone, a cotton T-shirt, a chocolate bar, a pair of trainers).","Research its supply chain using at least two reliable sources.","Record: where its raw materials come from, where it is made or assembled, and how it reaches consumers in the UAE.","Identify which drivers of globalisation (trade, technology, transport, communication) make this supply chain possible."], prompts:["The product I chose and my two sources","Where the raw materials come from","Where it is made or assembled, and how it reaches the UAE","The drivers of globalisation that make this supply chain possible"], success:"Strong answers trace a real supply chain across several stages and countries (e.g. cocoa grown in West Africa, processed in Europe, sold in Dubai) rather than naming a single country. They link specific stages to specific drivers, such as container shipping (transport), online ordering and tracking (technology/communication) and trade agreements (trade). For the top band, use two credible sources and comment on how interdependent the supply chain is: what would happen if one stage were disrupted?"},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"A family in Dubai orders a sofa online. It is designed in Sweden, made from wood grown in Eastern Europe and fabric woven in Asia, assembled in another country, shipped through Jebel Ali Port and delivered within a week.", steps:["In a structured paragraph (topic sentence, explanation, example, conclusion), explain how this extract illustrates globalisation.","Use at least THREE words from the Word Bank (e.g. globalisation, supply chain, interdependence) accurately.","Conclude by evaluating whether this level of interdependence makes economies stronger or more vulnerable."], model:"The sofa in the extract is a clear example of globalisation, because a single product connects designers, raw-material producers, manufacturers and consumers in several different countries. Its supply chain depends on trade between nations, on technology that allows the family to order online, and on transport infrastructure such as Jebel Ali Port. This shows the interdependence of modern economies: the family in Dubai relies on workers they will never meet, and those workers rely on consumers abroad for their income. On balance, I think this interdependence makes economies stronger by giving access to more goods and markets, but also more vulnerable, because a disruption at one stage, such as a blocked shipping route, can delay products worldwide.", rubric:[["1–2","Limited structure; little/no key vocabulary used."],["3–4","Basic paragraph structure; 1 key term used, possibly inaccurately."],["5–6","Clear paragraph structure; 2–3 key terms used mostly accurately; some evaluation."],["7–8","Well-structured paragraph; 3+ key terms used accurately; evaluation is balanced and well-justified."]], starters:["The sofa is an example of globalisation because ___.","Its supply chain depends on ___ and ___.","This shows interdependence because ___.","On balance, interdependence makes economies ___ because ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"The UAE in the Global Economy", steps:["In groups of 3–4, create an infographic showing how the UAE is connected to the global economy.","Include at least FOUR connections (e.g. ports and airports, tourism, foreign workers and investment, trade agreements, exports beyond oil).","For each connection, explain one way the UAE benefits and one way it depends on other countries.","Identify one global event that could disrupt one of these connections, and how the UAE might respond.","Present your infographic to the class in a 3-minute pitch. Use Word Bank vocabulary in your presentation."], rubric:[["1–2","Few connections identified; little explanation."],["3–4","Some connections identified; benefits and dependence described vaguely."],["5–6","Four clear connections with accurate benefits and dependence; disruption identified."],["7–8","Insightful, accurate connections; benefits and dependence analysed thoughtfully; realistic, well-reasoned response to disruption."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Driver of Globalisation?", prompt:"Sort each example under the driver of globalisation it best represents.", bins:["Trade","Technology & communication","Transport"], items:[["A trade agreement that reduces taxes on imports between two countries",0],["Container ships carrying thousands of goods across oceans",2],["Video calls letting a team in Dubai work with a team in Singapore",1],["A country exporting aluminium and importing cars",0],["A long-haul airline connecting cities on different continents",2],["An online shop delivering to customers in many countries",1],["Social media spreading a trend around the world in hours",1],["A free zone attracting foreign companies to set up offices",0]]},
  quiz:[["What is an 'economy'?",["A single company that sells goods abroad","The system by which a country or region produces, distributes and consumes goods and services","A type of tax paid on imports"],1],["Which definition best describes 'globalisation'?",["Countries closing their borders to trade","Only the spread of fast-food restaurants","The process by which economies, cultures and people become increasingly connected and interdependent"],2],["What does 'interdependence' mean?",["Countries or people relying on each other, so that changes in one affect the others","A country being completely self-sufficient","Two countries competing in sport"],0],["What is a 'supply chain'?",["A chain used to lock a warehouse","A list of a shop's prices","The sequence of steps and places involved in producing a product and delivering it"],2],["Which of these is a driver of globalisation?",["Improved transport, such as container shipping and air travel","Less use of the internet","Fewer trade agreements"],0],["Why has the UAE pursued economic diversification?",["To stop all trade with other countries","To reduce its reliance on a single sector, such as oil","To make products more expensive"],1],["In the sofa extract, what does Jebel Ali Port represent?",["A design studio","A raw material","Transport infrastructure that connects the UAE to global trade"],2],["What is one risk of a highly interdependent global economy?",["Consumers can buy more products","A disruption in one place can affect supply chains worldwide","Countries can specialise in what they do best"],1]],
  exit:"In one sentence, explain how ONE item you own connects you to people in another country.",
  copy:{facts:["An economy is the system by which goods and services are produced, distributed and consumed.","Globalisation is the growing connection and interdependence of economies, cultures and people, driven by trade, technology, transport and communication.","The UAE is a global trade and logistics hub and is diversifying its economy beyond oil."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we continue Unit 1 by weighing up the benefits and drawbacks of globalisation."
 },
 {
  id:"l2", lesson:2, title:"The Benefits and Drawbacks of Globalisation", unit:"Unit 1: Ethics and the Global Economy", art:"reason",
  dna:"Recap: in one sentence, define 'globalisation' from last lesson. Now think: name ONE way globalisation makes your life better, and ONE way it might cause harm to someone, somewhere.",
  dnaQs:["In one sentence, what is globalisation?","Name ONE way globalisation makes your life better.","Name ONE way globalisation might cause harm to someone, somewhere."],
  dnaHint:"Globalisation is the growing connection and interdependence of economies, cultures and people. A benefit could be access to a wide range of affordable products, jobs or cultural exchange. A harm could be low wages in factories, environmental damage from shipping, or local businesses struggling to compete.",
  intentions:["Understand the main economic, social, cultural and environmental benefits and drawbacks of globalisation.","Explore how globalisation can affect different groups of people in different ways.","Evaluate whether the benefits of globalisation outweigh its drawbacks, using ethical reasoning."],
  criteria:["I can explain benefits and drawbacks of globalisation across different areas of life.","I can analyse how globalisation creates 'winners' and 'losers' among different groups.","I can reach a balanced, justified judgement about globalisation."],
  concept:{key:"Global interactions", related:"Perspective, Consequences", soi:"Globalisation brings both opportunities and costs, and its ethical impact depends on whose perspective is considered.", context:"Globalisation and Sustainability — exploring the uneven impacts of global economic connection on people and the planet.", uae:"The UAE's open, multicultural society with residents from many nations, the UAE's commitment to preserving national heritage and Arabic identity alongside global influence, and UAE Net Zero 2050; links to Individuals & Societies and Sciences (environmental impact)."},
  words:[["Benefit","An advantage or positive effect gained from something."],["Drawback","A disadvantage or problem that makes something less acceptable."],["Outsourcing","When a company moves work or production to another company or country, often to reduce costs."],["Cultural homogenisation","The process by which local cultures become more similar to one another, often losing distinctive traditions."],["Exploitation","Treating people unfairly in order to benefit from their work, such as through very low pay or unsafe conditions."],["Perspective","A particular point of view shaped by a person's position, experience or interests."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Choose ONE real example of globalisation's impact (e.g. the growth of a manufacturing industry in an Asian country, global fast-food chains, the global tourism industry, international shipping).","Research it using at least two reliable sources.","Record: one benefit and one drawback of this example, and WHO experiences each.","Note one action taken (by a government, business or organisation) to reduce the drawback."], prompts:["The example I chose and my two sources","One benefit, and who experiences it","One drawback, and who experiences it","One action taken to reduce the drawback"], success:"Strong answers identify specific groups affected (e.g. factory workers, local shop owners, consumers, future generations) rather than saying 'people' in general, and recognise that the same development can benefit one group while harming another. For example, a garment industry can create jobs and lift incomes, but may also involve long hours or unsafe conditions. For the top band, use two credible sources and explain a real response, such as stronger safety laws or industry agreements."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"A large international clothing company opens a factory in a developing country. Thousands of local people gain jobs, but wages are low and hours are long. Meanwhile, in a wealthier country, a local clothing factory closes because it cannot compete on price.", steps:["In a structured paragraph (topic sentence, explanation, example, conclusion), analyse who benefits and who loses from the situation in the extract.","Use at least THREE words from the Word Bank (e.g. outsourcing, exploitation, perspective) accurately.","Conclude with a justified judgement: is this example of globalisation ethical overall?"], model:"The extract shows that globalisation can create both winners and losers, depending on whose perspective we consider. Through outsourcing, the company benefits from lower costs and consumers benefit from cheaper clothing, while thousands of workers in the developing country gain jobs that may not otherwise exist. However, low wages and long hours raise concerns about exploitation, and workers in the wealthier country lose their livelihoods when the local factory closes. In my judgement, this example is not fully ethical as it stands, because the company profits while workers carry most of the costs; it could become more ethical if the company paid fair wages and guaranteed safe, reasonable working conditions. (Other conclusions can earn full marks if they are well-justified.)", rubric:[["1–2","Limited structure; little/no key vocabulary used."],["3–4","Basic paragraph structure; 1 key term used, possibly inaccurately; one-sided."],["5–6","Clear paragraph structure; 2–3 key terms used mostly accurately; more than one perspective considered."],["7–8","Well-structured paragraph; 3+ key terms used accurately; multiple perspectives analysed; judgement is well-justified."]], starters:["From the perspective of ___, globalisation brings ___.","However, ___ may experience exploitation because ___.","Outsourcing benefits ___ but harms ___.","In my judgement, this is / is not ethical overall because ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"Globalisation Debate", steps:["In groups of 3–4, you will be assigned a side for the motion: \"This house believes the benefits of globalisation outweigh its drawbacks.\"","Prepare THREE arguments for your side, each supported by a real example from your research.","Anticipate TWO arguments the other side will make, and prepare rebuttals.","Include at least one argument that considers an ethical perspective (fairness, workers' rights, the environment or cultural identity).","Take part in a structured class debate, then write a short personal reflection on which side you found most convincing and why."], rubric:[["1–2","Arguments are underdeveloped and unsupported by examples."],["3–4","Basic arguments with limited examples; little rebuttal."],["5–6","Clear arguments supported by relevant examples; reasonable rebuttals; ethical perspective included."],["7–8","Sophisticated, well-evidenced arguments; strong rebuttals; ethical reasoning is insightful; reflection shows genuine engagement with both sides."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Benefit or Drawback?", prompt:"Sort each effect of globalisation: is it usually seen as a benefit or a drawback?", bins:["Benefit","Drawback"], items:[["Consumers can buy a wider range of goods at lower prices",0],["Workers in some factories face low pay and unsafe conditions",1],["New ideas, medicines and technologies spread quickly between countries",0],["Local traditions and small businesses struggle to survive",1],["Increased shipping and flights add to carbon emissions",1],["Foreign investment creates jobs in developing economies",0],["People from different cultures learn from and understand each other",0],["Jobs move abroad, leaving some communities without work",1]]},
  quiz:[["What is 'outsourcing'?",["Moving work or production to another company or country, often to reduce costs","Buying only local products","Closing a company completely"],0],["What does 'cultural homogenisation' mean?",["Celebrating many different cultures","Local cultures becoming more similar and losing distinctive traditions","A type of cooking"],1],["Which is an environmental drawback of globalisation?",["Cheaper products","Faster communication","Carbon emissions from transporting goods around the world"],2],["What does 'exploitation' mean in an economic context?",["Treating people unfairly to benefit from their work, such as through very low pay","Exploring new countries","Investing in a company"],0],["Why is it important to consider 'perspective' when judging globalisation?",["Because everyone is affected in exactly the same way","Because the same change can benefit one group and harm another","Because only governments' views matter"],1],["In the extract, who loses out when the local factory closes?",["Consumers buying cheaper clothes","The international company","Workers in the wealthier country"],2],["Which is an example of a benefit of globalisation?",["Medicines and technologies spreading quickly between countries","Jobs disappearing from a community","Unsafe factory conditions"],0],["How does the UAE balance global influence with national identity?",["By banning all foreign products","By promoting national heritage and the Arabic language alongside openness to the world","By refusing to trade with other countries"],1]],
  exit:"Complete this sentence: \"Globalisation is fair when ___, and unfair when ___.\"",
  copy:{facts:["Globalisation brings benefits such as cheaper goods, jobs, investment and the spread of ideas and technology.","Drawbacks include exploitation of workers, job losses in some communities, environmental damage and cultural homogenisation.","Whether globalisation is ethical depends on whose perspective we consider; it often creates both winners and losers."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we continue Unit 1 by exploring income inequality and why it matters ethically."
 },
 {
  id:"l3", lesson:3, title:"What Is Income Inequality?", unit:"Unit 1: Ethics and the Global Economy", art:"fair",
  dna:"Recap: in one sentence, name one benefit and one drawback of globalisation. Now think: if ten people shared a cake and one person took half of it, would that be fair? Write down what would make it fair or unfair.",
  dnaQs:["Name one benefit and one drawback of globalisation.","If one of ten people took half the cake, would that be fair?","What would make the sharing fair or unfair?"],
  dnaHint:"A benefit could be cheaper goods or new jobs; a drawback could be low wages or environmental harm. For the cake, a strong answer recognises that 'fair' does not always mean 'equal': it might depend on who baked the cake, who is hungriest, or whether everyone agreed the rules beforehand.",
  intentions:["Understand what income inequality is and how it can be measured.","Explore the causes and consequences of income inequality, within and between countries.","Evaluate the difference between equality and equity, and what a fair distribution of income might look like."],
  criteria:["I can define income inequality and explain how it is measured (e.g. the Gini coefficient).","I can explain causes and consequences of income inequality with examples.","I can evaluate different views on what makes a distribution of income fair."],
  concept:{key:"Fairness", related:"Equity, Development", soi:"Income inequality raises ethical questions about fairness, opportunity and our responsibilities to others in society and across the world.", context:"Fairness and Development — exploring how wealth and opportunity are distributed and what a just society owes its members.", uae:"Islamic values of social justice and care for those in need, including zakat and sadaqah; UAE social support programmes and initiatives such as the UAE's global humanitarian aid; links to Individuals & Societies (Economics) and Mathematics (data and statistics)."},
  words:[["Income","Money received regularly, for example from work (wages) or investments."],["Inequality","An unequal distribution of something, such as income, wealth or opportunity, between people or groups."],["Gini coefficient","A measure of income inequality from 0 (everyone has exactly the same income) to 1 (one person has all the income)."],["Equity","Fairness in how people are treated, which may mean giving more support to those who need it most."],["Poverty","The state of not having enough money or resources to meet basic needs."],["Social mobility","The ability of people to move between different income or social levels during their lives."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Choose TWO countries with different levels of income inequality (use a reliable source such as the World Bank or the UN Development Programme).","Research at least ONE cause of income inequality in each country (e.g. access to education, type of jobs available, tax systems, historical factors).","Record: how inequality is measured in your sources, and one consequence of inequality in each country.","Note one policy or programme either country uses to reduce inequality or poverty."], prompts:["The two countries I chose and my sources","How inequality is measured in my sources","One cause and one consequence of inequality in each country","One policy or programme used to reduce inequality or poverty"], success:"Strong answers use a recognised measure (such as the Gini coefficient) from a credible, dated source, and avoid sweeping claims about a whole population. Good causes include unequal access to quality education, differences between high-skill and low-skill jobs, and tax and welfare systems. Good consequences include reduced social mobility, health differences and social tension. For the top band, explain a real policy (e.g. free education, minimum wage laws, social support payments) and comment on how effective it may be."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"Two students are equally talented. One attends a well-funded school with private tutoring and a quiet place to study at home. The other attends an under-resourced school and works part-time to support their family. Years later, their incomes are very different.", steps:["In a structured paragraph (topic sentence, explanation, example, conclusion), explain how the extract shows the link between opportunity and income inequality.","Use at least THREE words from the Word Bank (e.g. inequality, equity, social mobility) accurately.","Conclude by explaining whether equality or equity would be the fairer response, and why."], model:"The extract shows that income inequality is often rooted in unequal opportunity, rather than differences in talent or effort. Although both students are equally able, one benefits from resources that the other lacks, which limits the second student's social mobility and leads to very different incomes later in life. Treating both students equally, by giving them identical support from now on, would not correct the disadvantage that already exists. I think equity is the fairer response, because giving extra support to the student facing more barriers, such as access to tutoring or study spaces, gives both a genuinely fair chance to reach their potential.", rubric:[["1–2","Limited structure; little/no key vocabulary used."],["3–4","Basic paragraph structure; 1 key term used, possibly inaccurately."],["5–6","Clear paragraph structure; 2–3 key terms used mostly accurately; equality/equity distinction attempted."],["7–8","Well-structured paragraph; 3+ key terms used accurately; equality/equity distinction is clear and the judgement is well-justified."]], starters:["The extract shows that income inequality is linked to ___.","Although both students are equally talented, ___.","This limits social mobility because ___.","I think equity / equality is fairer because ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"A Fairer Society Policy Proposal", steps:["In groups of 3–4, you are advisers to the government of a fictional country with high income inequality.","Propose TWO policies to reduce income inequality or increase social mobility (e.g. education, training, a minimum wage, social support).","For each policy, explain who it helps, how it would be funded, and one possible drawback or objection.","Explain which ethical principle (equality, equity, need, reward for effort) supports each policy.","Present your proposal to the class as a 3-minute briefing. Use Word Bank vocabulary in your presentation."], rubric:[["1–2","Policies are vague; little justification or ethical reasoning."],["3–4","Basic policies with limited detail; drawbacks named but not explored."],["5–6","Clear, realistic policies; who they help and drawbacks explained; ethical principle identified."],["7–8","Well-designed, realistic policies; drawbacks and funding thoughtfully addressed; ethical reasoning is insightful and convincing."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Equality or Equity?", prompt:"Sort each example: does it show equality (everyone gets the same) or equity (support matched to need)?", bins:["Equality (the same for all)","Equity (matched to need)"], items:[["Every student receives the same textbook",0],["A student with a visual impairment receives large-print materials",1],["Everyone pays the same flat fee for a service",0],["Families on low incomes receive extra support with school costs",1],["Each worker in a team receives an identical bonus",0],["A scholarship is offered to talented students who cannot afford fees",1],["All citizens have one vote each in an election",0],["Extra tutoring is provided for students who have fallen behind",1]]},
  quiz:[["What is income inequality?",["An unequal distribution of income between people or groups","When everyone earns exactly the same","A tax paid on income"],0],["On the Gini coefficient, what does a score of 0 mean?",["One person has all the income","Everyone has exactly the same income","The country has no economy"],1],["What is the difference between equality and equity?",["They mean exactly the same thing","Equity means giving nobody any support","Equality gives everyone the same; equity matches support to need"],2],["What does 'social mobility' mean?",["Moving house frequently","Using social media on the move","The ability of people to move between income or social levels during their lives"],2],["Which of these is a common cause of income inequality?",["Unequal access to quality education","Everyone having the same skills","Identical wages for all jobs"],0],["In the extract, why do the two students end up with different incomes?",["One student is far more talented","They had unequal opportunities and resources","One student did not want to work"],1],["Which Islamic practice is linked to reducing poverty?",["Zakat, a compulsory charitable giving","Charging high interest on loans","Keeping all wealth private"],0],["Which is a possible consequence of high income inequality?",["Greater social tension and reduced social mobility","Everyone having the same opportunities","No poverty at all"],0]],
  exit:"In one sentence, explain whether you think a fair society needs equal incomes, equal opportunities, or both.",
  copy:{facts:["Income inequality is the unequal distribution of income between people or groups; it can be measured using the Gini coefficient (0 = complete equality, 1 = complete inequality).","Causes include unequal access to education, differences in jobs and skills, and tax and welfare systems; consequences include poverty, reduced social mobility and social tension.","Equality means treating everyone the same; equity means matching support to need. Islamic values such as zakat encourage care for those in need."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we continue Unit 1 by exploring fair trade and how it aims to make global trade more just."
 },
 {
  id:"l4", lesson:4, title:"What Is Fair Trade?", unit:"Unit 1: Ethics and the Global Economy", art:"community",
  dna:"Recap: in one sentence, explain the difference between equality and equity. Now think: a cocoa farmer receives only a very small share of the price you pay for a chocolate bar. Write down who you think receives the rest, and whether this seems fair.",
  dnaQs:["In one sentence, what is the difference between equality and equity?","If the cocoa farmer receives only a small share, who do you think receives the rest?","Does this seem fair? Why or why not?"],
  dnaHint:"Equality means everyone gets the same; equity means support is matched to need. The rest of the chocolate price is shared between manufacturers, transport companies, brands, advertisers, retailers and taxes. A thoughtful answer asks whether the farmer, who does much of the hardest work, is fairly rewarded.",
  intentions:["Understand what fair trade is and how fair trade certification works.","Explore how fair trade aims to support producers in developing countries.","Evaluate the strengths and limitations of fair trade as a response to unfair global trade."],
  criteria:["I can explain what fair trade is, including minimum prices and a social premium.","I can describe how fair trade can benefit producers and their communities.","I can evaluate the strengths and limitations of fair trade with balanced reasoning."],
  concept:{key:"Fairness", related:"Justice, Global interactions", soi:"Fair trade challenges unjust trading relationships by seeking fair prices, decent working conditions and sustainable development for producers.", context:"Fairness and Development — exploring how trade rules and consumer choices can promote or undermine justice for producers.", uae:"Islamic teachings on honest, fair dealing in trade and paying workers their due (the Prophet's ﷺ guidance to give the worker his wage before his sweat dries); the UAE's historic identity as a trading nation; links to Individuals & Societies (Economics) and Design (product labelling)."},
  words:[["Fair trade","A trading approach that aims to give producers in developing countries fair prices, decent working conditions and better terms of trade."],["Producer","A person or organisation that grows or makes goods, such as a farmer or craftsperson."],["Minimum price","A guaranteed price paid to producers that aims to cover their costs, even if market prices fall."],["Premium","An extra sum of money paid on top of the price, which producers invest in their communities or businesses."],["Certification","An official process that checks a product meets agreed standards, shown by a label or mark."],["Cooperative","An organisation owned and run jointly by its members, such as a group of farmers, who share the benefits."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Choose ONE commonly fair-traded product (e.g. coffee, cocoa, bananas, tea, cotton, sugar).","Research how fair trade works for this product using at least two reliable sources (e.g. the Fairtrade Foundation, the World Fair Trade Organization).","Record: what standards producers must meet, what the minimum price and premium are for, and one example of how a premium has been used.","Note one criticism or limitation of fair trade for this product."], prompts:["The product I chose and my two sources","The standards producers must meet","What the minimum price and the premium are for, with one example of premium use","One criticism or limitation"], success:"Strong answers explain the purpose of the minimum price (a safety net when market prices fall) and the premium (extra money that producer groups decide how to invest, such as in schools, clean water or better farming equipment). Good criticisms include: certification costs can be difficult for the poorest farmers; only a portion of a product may be sold on fair trade terms; and fair trade products can cost consumers more. For the top band, use two credible sources and give a specific, real example of premium use."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"A farmers' cooperative growing coffee in the mountains joins a fair trade scheme. When world coffee prices fall sharply, they still receive the minimum price. They vote to use their premium to build a water well and to buy equipment that improves the quality of their crop.", steps:["In a structured paragraph (topic sentence, explanation, example, conclusion), explain how fair trade benefits the cooperative in the extract.","Use at least THREE words from the Word Bank (e.g. cooperative, minimum price, premium) accurately.","Conclude by evaluating ONE limitation of fair trade that the extract does not mention."], model:"The extract shows how fair trade can protect producers and support long-term development in their communities. Because the cooperative receives a guaranteed minimum price, the farmers are protected when world coffee prices fall sharply, so they can still cover their costs and plan for the future. In addition, the premium gives them money to invest in priorities they choose themselves, such as a water well that benefits the whole community and equipment that improves the quality of their coffee. However, one limitation is that certification can be costly and demanding, so the very poorest farmers, who are not part of an organised cooperative, may struggle to join such schemes and benefit at all.", rubric:[["1–2","Limited structure; little/no key vocabulary used."],["3–4","Basic paragraph structure; 1 key term used, possibly inaccurately."],["5–6","Clear paragraph structure; 2–3 key terms used mostly accurately; a limitation identified."],["7–8","Well-structured paragraph; 3+ key terms used accurately; limitation is relevant and well-explained."]], starters:["Fair trade benefits the cooperative because ___.","The minimum price protects the farmers when ___.","The premium allows them to ___.","However, one limitation of fair trade is ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"Fair Trade Awareness Campaign", steps:["In groups of 3–4, design an awareness campaign to help students and families in the UAE understand fair trade.","Choose ONE fair trade product and explain clearly how fair trade affects the people who produce it.","Include balanced information: at least two benefits and one honest limitation of fair trade.","Create a campaign resource (e.g. a poster, a short video script, or a social media post series) and suggest one practical action people could take.","Present your campaign to the class in a 3-minute pitch. Use Word Bank vocabulary in your presentation."], rubric:[["1–2","Campaign is vague or inaccurate; little explanation of fair trade."],["3–4","Basic campaign with limited detail; one-sided."],["5–6","Clear, accurate campaign with benefits and a limitation; practical action suggested."],["7–8","Persuasive, accurate and balanced campaign; insightful explanation of impact on producers; realistic and well-targeted action."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Fair Trade Principle or Not?", prompt:"Sort each statement: is it a fair trade principle, or does it go against fair trade?", bins:["Fair trade principle","Goes against fair trade"], items:[["Paying producers a minimum price that aims to cover their costs",0],["Using child labour to reduce costs",1],["Paying a premium that communities can invest as they choose",0],["Paying farmers as little as possible to maximise profit",1],["Supporting farmers to form democratic cooperatives",0],["Encouraging environmentally sustainable farming",0],["Ignoring unsafe working conditions",1],["Long-term, transparent trading relationships with producers",0]]},
  quiz:[["What is the main aim of fair trade?",["To make products as cheap as possible","To give producers fair prices, decent working conditions and better terms of trade","To stop all international trade"],1],["What is the purpose of a fair trade minimum price?",["To act as a safety net that aims to cover producers' costs when market prices fall","To make shops charge more","To pay advertising costs"],0],["What is a fair trade 'premium' used for?",["Paying the shop owner","Advertising the product","Investment chosen by producers, such as in community projects or better equipment"],2],["What is a 'cooperative'?",["A single large company","A government department","An organisation owned and run jointly by its members, who share the benefits"],2],["What does fair trade 'certification' show?",["That a product meets agreed fair trade standards","That a product is the cheapest available","That a product was made in the UAE"],0],["In the extract, how did the cooperative use its premium?",["To pay a famous brand","To build a water well and buy equipment","To lower the price of coffee"],1],["Which is a genuine limitation of fair trade?",["It always makes producers poorer","It bans farmers from forming groups","Certification can be costly, so the poorest farmers may struggle to join"],2],["Which Islamic teaching links to fair trade?",["Give the worker his wage before his sweat dries","Always buy the cheapest product","Trade should only happen locally"],0]],
  exit:"Name ONE fair trade product you could choose instead of a non-certified one, and explain who it helps.",
  copy:{facts:["Fair trade aims to give producers in developing countries fair prices, decent working conditions and better terms of trade.","Key features include a minimum price (a safety net) and a premium that producer groups invest in their communities or businesses.","Fair trade has limitations, such as certification costs and higher prices, but it offers consumers a way to support fairer trade."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we continue Unit 1 by exploring the ethical consequences of our everyday consumer choices."
 },
 {
  id:"l5", lesson:5, title:"The Ethical Consequences of Our Consumer Choices", unit:"Unit 1: Ethics and the Global Economy", art:"eye",
  dna:"Recap: in one sentence, explain what a fair trade premium is. Now think: the last time you bought clothes, food or a gadget, what influenced your choice most (price, brand, quality, friends, adverts, ethics)? Write down your top TWO reasons.",
  dnaQs:["In one sentence, what is a fair trade premium?","What were your top TWO reasons for the last thing you bought?","Did ethics (people or planet) play any part in your choice?"],
  dnaHint:"A fair trade premium is extra money paid on top of the price, which producer groups invest in their communities. Most people choose on price, brand or quality. An honest answer is fine: the point is to notice how rarely we think about who made a product and its impact on the planet.",
  intentions:["Understand what is meant by ethical consumerism and the consequences of consumer choices.","Explore how consumer choices affect workers, communities and the environment across global supply chains.","Evaluate the power and limits of individual consumers in bringing about ethical change."],
  criteria:["I can explain what ethical consumerism means, with examples.","I can analyse the social and environmental consequences of a consumer choice along a supply chain.","I can evaluate how much responsibility individuals, businesses and governments each have for ethical consumption."],
  concept:{key:"Responsibility", related:"Consequences, Sustainability", soi:"Every purchase has consequences for people and the planet, so consumers share responsibility for the impacts of the global economy.", context:"Globalisation and Sustainability — exploring how the choices of individuals connect to working conditions and environmental impacts worldwide.", uae:"Islamic teachings against waste and extravagance (israf) and humans' role as stewards (khalifa) of the Earth; the UAE's sustainability focus, including the 2023 Year of Sustainability and UAE Net Zero 2050; links to Sciences (environmental impact) and Design (product life cycle)."},
  words:[["Consumer","A person who buys goods or services for their own use."],["Ethical consumerism","Choosing products and services that minimise harm to people, animals and the environment."],["Fast fashion","Cheap clothing produced rapidly in large quantities to follow the latest trends, often worn only briefly."],["Boycott","Refusing to buy from or use a company or product, as a form of protest."],["Sustainability","Meeting present needs without damaging the ability of future generations to meet their own."],["Consumerism","A culture or belief that buying more goods and services is desirable and leads to happiness."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Choose ONE consumer issue (e.g. fast fashion, electronic waste, single-use plastics, food waste, conflict minerals in electronics).","Research it using at least two reliable sources.","Record: the social consequences (for workers or communities) and environmental consequences of this issue.","Identify one action consumers can take, and one action a business or government has taken, to address it."], prompts:["The issue I chose and my two sources","The social consequences for workers or communities","The environmental consequences","One action consumers can take, and one action a business or government has taken"], success:"Strong answers make clear links between a consumer behaviour and a real consequence elsewhere in the supply chain. A well-known example is the 2013 Rana Plaza factory collapse in Bangladesh, in which more than a thousand garment workers died, which led to new building-safety agreements in the clothing industry. Strong answers distinguish between individual action (e.g. buying fewer, better-quality items, repairing, recycling) and system-level action (e.g. laws, industry standards). For the top band, use two credible sources and evaluate how effective the actions are."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"A teenager sees an online advert for a trendy T-shirt that costs less than a meal. They buy five in different colours, wear each one only a few times, and throw them away when the trend changes a few months later.", steps:["In a structured paragraph (topic sentence, explanation, example, conclusion), analyse the ethical consequences of the teenager's choices.","Use at least THREE words from the Word Bank (e.g. fast fashion, sustainability, consumerism) accurately.","Conclude by evaluating how much responsibility lies with the teenager, compared with the brand and the government."], model:"The teenager's choices are a typical example of fast fashion, and they have ethical consequences far beyond the shop. Such a low price can suggest that workers somewhere in the supply chain may be paid very little or work in poor conditions, while producing and quickly discarding clothes wastes water, energy and materials, which goes against the principle of sustainability. The advert also reflects a culture of consumerism that encourages people to buy more than they need. In my view, the teenager has some responsibility, because they could choose to buy less and keep clothes longer; however, brands carry greater responsibility for how their products are made and marketed, and governments are responsible for setting and enforcing laws that protect workers and the environment.", rubric:[["1–2","Limited structure; little/no key vocabulary used."],["3–4","Basic paragraph structure; 1 key term used, possibly inaccurately."],["5–6","Clear paragraph structure; 2–3 key terms used mostly accurately; responsibility discussed."],["7–8","Well-structured paragraph; 3+ key terms used accurately; consequences analysed for people and planet; responsibility is weighed in a balanced, justified way."]], starters:["The teenager's choices are an example of ___ because ___.","One social consequence is ___.","One environmental consequence is ___, which goes against sustainability.","The teenager has some responsibility, but ___ also ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"Ethical Shopper's Guide", steps:["In groups of 3–4, create an 'Ethical Shopper's Guide' for students in the UAE.","Choose THREE product categories (e.g. clothing, electronics, food, cosmetics).","For each category, explain one ethical concern and give two practical, affordable actions students can take.","Include a short section evaluating the limits of individual action and what businesses or governments should do.","Present your guide to the class in a 3-minute pitch. Use Word Bank vocabulary in your presentation."], rubric:[["1–2","Guide is vague; concerns and actions are unclear or unrealistic."],["3–4","Basic guide with limited detail; actions are generic."],["5–6","Clear guide with accurate concerns and practical actions; limits of individual action considered."],["7–8","Insightful, accurate and practical guide; actions are realistic for students; evaluation of individual versus system responsibility is thoughtful."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Ethical Choice or Not?", prompt:"Sort each consumer behaviour: is it an ethical choice, or one that is likely to cause harm?", bins:["More ethical choice","Likely to cause harm"], items:[["Repairing a phone instead of replacing it",0],["Buying several cheap outfits to wear once each",1],["Choosing products with a recognised fair trade label",0],["Throwing old electronics in the general rubbish",1],["Buying second-hand or swapping clothes with friends",0],["Ordering far more food than you need and wasting it",1],["Researching how a brand treats its workers before buying",0],["Buying a product only because an influencer promoted it",1]]},
  quiz:[["What is 'ethical consumerism'?",["Choosing products that minimise harm to people, animals and the environment","Buying the cheapest product available","Only shopping online"],0],["What is 'fast fashion'?",["Clothing for runners","Expensive designer clothing that lasts many years","Cheap clothing produced rapidly to follow trends, often worn only briefly"],2],["What is a 'boycott'?",["A type of advert","Refusing to buy from a company or product as a form of protest","A discount on clothing"],1],["What does 'sustainability' mean?",["Meeting present needs without damaging the ability of future generations to meet theirs","Using as many resources as possible","Making products quickly"],0],["Which event in 2013 led to new building-safety agreements in the clothing industry?",["The opening of a new shopping mall","The Rana Plaza factory collapse in Bangladesh","A fashion show in Paris"],1],["Which Islamic concept warns against waste and extravagance?",["Ta'awun","Shura","Israf"],2],["Which is the most balanced view of responsibility for ethical consumption?",["Only consumers are responsible","Only governments are responsible","Consumers, businesses and governments all share responsibility"],2],["Which action best reduces the environmental impact of clothing?",["Buying fewer, better-quality items and keeping them longer","Buying new clothes every week","Throwing away clothes when trends change"],0]],
  exit:"Write ONE change you could realistically make to your shopping habits this month, and the consequence it would have.",
  copy:{facts:["Ethical consumerism means choosing products that minimise harm to people, animals and the environment.","Consumer choices, such as fast fashion, have consequences along global supply chains for workers' rights and the planet.","Responsibility is shared: consumers can choose wisely, but businesses and governments must also act. Islam warns against waste (israf)."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we complete Unit 1 by exploring socially responsible investment."
 },
 {
  id:"l6", lesson:6, title:"Socially Responsible Investment", unit:"Unit 1: Ethics and the Global Economy", art:"business",
  dna:"Unit 1 vocabulary check! Define each term as precisely as you can: Globalisation, Interdependence, Income inequality, Fair trade, Ethical consumerism. Then think: if you were given money to invest, would you care what the company does, or only how much profit it makes?",
  dnaQs:["Globalisation and Interdependence — what does each mean?","Income inequality, Fair trade and Ethical consumerism — what does each mean?","If you invested money, would you care what the company does, or only the profit?"],
  dnaHint:"Globalisation: growing connection and interdependence of economies and people. Interdependence: relying on each other so changes in one affect others. Income inequality: unequal distribution of income. Fair trade: fair prices and conditions for producers. Ethical consumerism: choosing products that minimise harm. For the investment question, a thoughtful answer considers both profit and the company's impact on people and the planet.",
  intentions:["Understand what socially responsible investment (SRI) is and how it works.","Explore investment approaches such as ethical screening, ESG criteria and Islamic (Sharia-compliant) investment.","Evaluate whether investors have a moral responsibility for what their money supports, drawing together learning from across Unit 1."],
  criteria:["I can explain what socially responsible investment is, including screening and ESG.","I can compare different approaches to responsible investment, including Islamic finance principles.","I can evaluate investors' moral responsibilities and connect them to ideas from across Unit 1."],
  concept:{key:"Responsibility", related:"Ethics, Sustainability", soi:"Investors share moral responsibility for what their money supports, and can use investment to promote a fairer and more sustainable global economy.", context:"Globalisation and Sustainability — exploring how financial decisions shape businesses, communities and the environment.", uae:"Islamic finance principles (avoiding riba (interest) and investment in prohibited activities), the UAE's role as a leading centre for Islamic finance and sukuk, and UAE Net Zero 2050 and green finance initiatives; links to Individuals & Societies (Business and Economics)."},
  words:[["Investment","Putting money into a business, project or asset with the expectation of a financial return."],["Socially responsible investment (SRI)","Investing in a way that considers social, environmental and ethical impacts, as well as financial returns."],["ESG","Environmental, Social and Governance criteria used to assess how responsibly a company behaves."],["Ethical screening","Including or excluding companies from investment based on ethical criteria, such as avoiding harmful industries."],["Shareholder","A person or organisation that owns shares (part ownership) in a company."],["Sharia-compliant investment","Investment that follows Islamic principles, such as avoiding interest (riba) and prohibited industries."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion A — Knowing and Understanding", steps:["Choose ONE approach to responsible investment: ESG investing, ethical screening, impact investing, green bonds, or Sharia-compliant investment (including sukuk).","Research it using at least two reliable sources.","Record: how this approach works, what it includes or excludes, and why investors choose it.","Note one example of this approach in practice (ideally linked to the UAE or the Gulf region) and one criticism of it."], prompts:["The approach I chose and my two sources","How it works, and what it includes or excludes","Why investors choose it, and one example in practice","One criticism of this approach"], success:"Strong answers explain the approach accurately: for example, Sharia-compliant investment avoids interest-based lending and sectors such as alcohol and gambling; ESG investing assesses companies on environmental, social and governance factors; green bonds raise money for environmental projects. Good criticisms include 'greenwashing' (companies exaggerating their responsible behaviour) and the difficulty of measuring impact consistently. For the top band, use two credible sources and give a specific, real example."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"Reflect on Unit 1: Lesson 1 (economy and globalisation), Lesson 2 (benefits and drawbacks of globalisation), Lesson 3 (income inequality), Lesson 4 (fair trade), Lesson 5 (consumer choices) and Lesson 6 (socially responsible investment).", steps:["Write a structured paragraph (topic sentence, three connections, conclusion) explaining how individuals can act ethically within the global economy.","Use at least FOUR words from across Unit 1's Word Bank (any lesson) accurately.","Conclude with your own justified view: who has the greatest power to make the global economy fairer — consumers, investors, businesses or governments?"], model:"This unit has shown that, because of globalisation and interdependence, our economic choices affect people and the environment far beyond our own country. As consumers, we can practise ethical consumerism by choosing fair trade products and avoiding fast fashion, which supports producers and helps reduce exploitation (Lessons 4–5). As future investors, we can use socially responsible investment, such as ESG or Sharia-compliant approaches, to direct money towards businesses that treat people fairly and protect the planet (Lesson 6). These choices also connect to income inequality, because fairer wages and responsible investment can widen opportunity rather than concentrate wealth (Lesson 3). In my view, governments have the greatest power, because they set the laws that all businesses must follow; however, consumers and investors create demand that encourages businesses to change, so real progress needs all of them acting together.", rubric:[["1–2","Limited structure; little/no key vocabulary used; no real connections made."],["3–4","Basic paragraph structure; 1–2 key terms used; connections are vague."],["5–6","Clear paragraph structure; 3 key terms used mostly accurately; reasonable connections across lessons."],["7–8","Well-structured paragraph; 4+ key terms used accurately across multiple lessons; connections are insightful; conclusion is well-justified."]], starters:["Because of globalisation, our economic choices ___.","As consumers, we can ___.","As investors, we can ___, such as ___.","In my view, ___ have the greatest power because ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task (Unit Showcase)", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"Responsible Investment Fund Pitch", steps:["In groups of 3–4, imagine you are managing a fictional investment fund for young people in the UAE.","Write a clear set of investment principles for your fund (what you will invest in, what you will exclude, and why), drawing on ESG, ethical screening and/or Islamic finance principles.","Choose THREE types of business or project your fund would support (e.g. renewable energy, fair trade producers, affordable healthcare) and justify each using ideas from across Unit 1.","Identify one trade-off your fund faces (e.g. lower short-term returns, risk of greenwashing) and explain how you would manage it.","Pitch your fund to the class as a 4-minute 'investor presentation'. The class votes on which fund is most convincing and ethically justified."], rubric:[["1–2","Investment principles are vague; little connection to unit learning."],["3–4","Basic principles and choices; limited justification; trade-off named but not addressed."],["5–6","Clear principles and well-chosen investments; justified using unit ideas; trade-off identified and addressed."],["7–8","Insightful, coherent principles; investments justified convincingly across multiple Unit 1 lessons; trade-off analysed thoughtfully and managed realistically."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Which Lesson Does It Connect To?", prompt:"Sort each idea into the part of Unit 1 it comes from.", bins:["Globalisation & inequality","Fair trade & consumer choices","Responsible investment"], items:[["Supply chains that connect producers and consumers in many countries",0],["The Gini coefficient as a measure of how income is shared",0],["A minimum price and premium paid to a farmers' cooperative",1],["Repairing a phone instead of buying a new one",1],["Assessing companies on environmental, social and governance criteria",2],["Excluding industries such as gambling from an investment fund",2],["Equity: matching support to need",0],["Avoiding fast fashion to reduce waste",1]]},
  quiz:[["What is socially responsible investment (SRI)?",["Investing only in the companies with the highest profits","Investing in a way that considers social, environmental and ethical impacts as well as financial returns","Giving money to charity with no return"],1],["What does ESG stand for?",["Economic, Social and Global","Ethical, Sustainable and Green","Environmental, Social and Governance"],2],["What is 'ethical screening'?",["Including or excluding companies based on ethical criteria","Watching films about ethics","Checking a company's share price daily"],0],["Which is a principle of Sharia-compliant investment?",["Investing heavily in gambling companies","Avoiding interest (riba) and prohibited industries","Charging the highest possible interest"],1],["What is a 'shareholder'?",["A person who works in a shop","A government tax officer","A person or organisation that owns part of a company through shares"],2],["What is 'greenwashing'?",["When a company exaggerates how environmentally responsible it is","Cleaning a factory with eco-friendly products","Investing in renewable energy"],0],["How does responsible investment connect to fair trade and ethical consumerism?",["It has no connection","All three use economic choices to support fairer treatment of people and the planet","They are all government laws"],1],["Which UAE commitment links to green investment?",["Ending all trade","Banning foreign companies","UAE Net Zero 2050"],2]],
  exit:"Write ONE principle you would follow if you invested money in the future, and explain which Unit 1 idea it comes from.",
  copy:{facts:["Socially responsible investment considers social, environmental and ethical impacts alongside financial returns, using tools such as ESG criteria and ethical screening.","Sharia-compliant investment avoids interest (riba) and prohibited industries; the UAE is a leading centre for Islamic finance.","Consumers, investors, businesses and governments all share responsibility for making the global economy fairer and more sustainable."]},
  next:"Unit 1: Ethics and the Global Economy is complete! Next, we begin Unit 2 of the Grade 10 Moral Education programme."
 }
];
